1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,170
Loneliness feels like a personal failure.

2
00:00:02,170 --> 00:00:04,820
Like something you'd be
embarrassed to admit.

3
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:11,340
Like evidence that you did something wrong, or that there's something
a bit off about you that other people can sense.

4
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:13,680
It isn't any of those things.

5
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:15,840
Loneliness is an alarm.

6
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:27,520
It's a piece of biological equipment, it works exactly the way it was designed to work, and it is
doing its job right now, on purpose, because two hundred thousand years ago being alone would have killed you.

7
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:31,280
So the question isn't whether
ancient humans got lonely.

8
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:33,900
The question is what the alarm was for.

9
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:37,860
Start with something your body
does that nobody finds shameful.

10
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:39,200
You get hungry.

11
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:41,540
That's not a character flaw.

12
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:47,620
It's a signal — an unpleasant one, deliberately
unpleasant, because a pleasant signal wouldn't work.

13
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:52,760
Your body has a thing it needs, and it
makes you uncomfortable until you go and get it.

14
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:55,110
Thirst does the same.

15
00:00:55,110 --> 00:01:03,460
So does pain: an alarm that something is damaging you, loud enough that
you can't ignore it and go back to what you were doing.

16
00:01:03,460 --> 00:01:06,360
Loneliness is built on the same principle.

17
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:12,610
It is the signal that fires when your social connections
drop below what your body has decided you need.

18
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:16,720
It is unpleasant for exactly the
same reason hunger is unpleasant.

19
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:20,430
And like hunger, it doesn't care
about your opinion of it.

20
00:01:20,430 --> 00:01:27,880
You can be perfectly aware that you're fine, that nothing is
wrong, that you have people — and still feel it.

21
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:33,460
The researcher who spent his career on
this, John Cacioppo, made the comparison directly.

22
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:36,450
Hunger protects your body from starvation.

23
00:01:36,450 --> 00:01:39,220
Thirst protects it from dehydration.

24
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:41,970
Physical pain protects it from injury.

25
00:01:41,970 --> 00:01:44,720
Loneliness protects it
from something else.

26
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:50,100
And to understand what, you have to look at
what happened to a human being on their own.

27
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:54,070
Picture the situation your body
is actually built for.

28
00:01:54,070 --> 00:01:55,740
You're one human.

29
00:01:55,740 --> 00:02:02,180
You're on the ground, in the open, in
Africa, a couple of hundred thousand years ago.

30
00:02:02,180 --> 00:02:03,890
You are not fast.

31
00:02:03,890 --> 00:02:05,260
You are not strong.

32
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:07,020
Your teeth are a joke.

33
00:02:07,020 --> 00:02:08,900
You cannot see in the dark.

34
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:11,700
On your own, you are not a predator.

35
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:13,490
You are lunch.

36
00:02:13,490 --> 00:02:15,350
A group solves this.

37
00:02:15,350 --> 00:02:18,260
In a group, someone is
awake while you sleep.

38
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:21,490
Someone else spotted the thing in
the grass before you did.

39
00:02:21,490 --> 00:02:27,120
Food gets shared, so a bad hunting
day is survivable rather than fatal.

40
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:40,750
If you break a leg, somebody brings you water for six weeks while it heals — and we know that happened, because
the archaeological record is full of healed serious injuries in people who could not possibly have fed themselves during the healing.

41
00:02:40,750 --> 00:02:43,400
That last detail is worth stopping on.

42
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:47,530
A healed broken femur in a
fossil is evidence of somebody.

43
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:49,120
Somebody stayed.

44
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:51,200
Now remove the group.

45
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:54,250
You are not going to
be killed by loneliness.

46
00:02:54,250 --> 00:03:06,070
You are going to be killed within a few weeks by the first infection, the first bad fall, the first
night you fell asleep with nobody watching, or the first stretch of days without food and nobody to share theirs.

47
00:03:06,070 --> 00:03:08,450
Being alone was not sad.

48
00:03:08,450 --> 00:03:09,730
It was terminal.

49
00:03:09,730 --> 00:03:17,240
So a body that produced a loud, horrible, unignorable alarm at
the first sign of isolation would keep its owner alive.

50
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:20,030
And that body is the
one you're sitting in.

51
00:03:20,030 --> 00:03:24,590
Here's where it gets specific, because
loneliness doesn't just make you sad.

52
00:03:24,590 --> 00:03:26,600
It changes what your body does.

53
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:28,930
Lonely people are more vigilant.

54
00:03:28,930 --> 00:03:30,030
Measurably.

55
00:03:30,030 --> 00:03:41,660
In experiments, people scoring high on loneliness detect social threat faster — an unfriendly face in
a crowd, a hostile tone — and they detect it in things that aren't actually threatening.

56
00:03:41,660 --> 00:03:44,020
Their threat-detection is turned up.

57
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:45,540
Which makes sense.

58
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:50,800
A human separated from the group is in danger,
and a body in danger should be scanning.

59
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:54,630
And there's a second effect that
fits the first one perfectly.

60
00:03:54,630 --> 00:03:57,100
Lonely people sleep worse.

61
00:03:57,100 --> 00:04:00,680
Not fewer hours, necessarily
— but more fragmented.

62
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:03,280
More micro-awakenings through the night.

63
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:06,490
Brief surfacings that they don't
even remember in the morning.

64
00:04:06,490 --> 00:04:08,840
Think about what that means.

65
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:11,770
Your body has worked out it
doesn't have a group around it.

66
00:04:11,770 --> 00:04:20,120
And so it stops sleeping deeply, because sleeping deeply without someone watching
is the single most dangerous thing a defenceless animal can do.

67
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:21,520
It is checking.

68
00:04:21,520 --> 00:04:22,760
All night.

69
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:24,380
Just in case.

70
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:32,310
You may recognise that from a certain feeling of waking up at three in
the morning for no reason after a period of feeling disconnected from everyone.

71
00:04:32,310 --> 00:04:33,960
That's not a coincidence.

72
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:35,580
That's the same system.

73
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:37,940
So did ancient humans feel this?

74
00:04:37,940 --> 00:04:43,520
Almost certainly — but far less often than
you do, because the opportunity barely existed.

75
00:04:43,520 --> 00:04:48,320
A hunter-gatherer band is usually somewhere
between twenty and fifty people.

76
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:57,990
Those bands sit inside a wider network of related groups that meet, trade, marry
across and visit, running to a few hundred people you might know by name.

77
00:04:57,990 --> 00:05:01,760
And within that, essentially
nothing happened alone.

78
00:05:01,760 --> 00:05:03,570
You slept in a group.

79
00:05:03,570 --> 00:05:05,200
You ate in a group.

80
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:08,750
Tools got made sitting next
to other people making tools.

81
00:05:08,750 --> 00:05:15,090
Children were raised by a rotating cast of adults,
not by two exhausted parents in a separate building.

82
00:05:15,090 --> 00:05:17,300
Giving birth was surrounded.

83
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:19,250
Dying was surrounded.

84
00:05:19,250 --> 00:05:32,120
The state you experience regularly — being in a room by yourself for hours, with the door shut, with nobody
knowing or particularly minding where you are — was close to unavailable for almost the whole of human existence.

85
00:05:32,120 --> 00:05:36,490
When it did happen, it usually
meant something had gone badly wrong.

86
00:05:36,490 --> 00:05:37,580
You were lost.

87
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:38,810
You'd been thrown out.

88
00:05:38,810 --> 00:05:40,220
Everyone else was dead.

89
00:05:40,220 --> 00:05:42,850
Which is why the alarm is so loud.

90
00:05:42,850 --> 00:05:44,830
It was never meant to go off often.

91
00:05:44,830 --> 00:05:46,570
It was built for emergencies.

92
00:05:46,570 --> 00:05:48,910
Now the thing that trips everybody up.

93
00:05:48,910 --> 00:05:52,000
Loneliness is not about how
many people are near you.

94
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,070
You can be lonely in a full house.

95
00:05:54,070 --> 00:06:01,630
You can be lonely at a party, in a relationship, in an
office of forty people, in a family group chat that never stops.

96
00:06:01,630 --> 00:06:06,150
And people who live genuinely alone
are frequently not lonely at all.

97
00:06:06,150 --> 00:06:08,920
Because the alarm is not counting bodies.

98
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:14,510
It is measuring the gap between the connection you
have and the connection you expect to have.

99
00:06:14,510 --> 00:06:17,140
Which is why it can fire in a crowd.

100
00:06:17,140 --> 00:06:26,390
Being surrounded by people who don't know you is, to a system built for a
band of forty who knew everything about you, a strange and slightly alarming signal.

101
00:06:26,390 --> 00:06:27,980
Everyone is here.

102
00:06:27,980 --> 00:06:29,720
Nobody is with you.

103
00:06:29,720 --> 00:06:36,950
And it is why the alarm can be quiet in a cabin in the woods,
if the person in the cabin has three people who would drop everything for them.

104
00:06:36,950 --> 00:06:41,730
The old measure was: does this group
have my back if something goes wrong?

105
00:06:41,730 --> 00:06:44,610
That is still the question
your body is asking.

106
00:06:44,610 --> 00:06:46,760
It has just got much harder to answer.

107
00:06:46,760 --> 00:06:51,630
And now the cruel part, which is the reason
loneliness is so hard to get out of.

108
00:06:51,630 --> 00:06:53,890
Hunger makes you seek food.

109
00:06:53,890 --> 00:06:56,630
Thirst makes you seek water.

110
00:06:56,630 --> 00:07:00,610
You'd expect loneliness to simply
make you seek people.

111
00:07:00,610 --> 00:07:02,300
It does, at first.

112
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:06,010
But it also does something else, and
the two work against each other.

113
00:07:06,010 --> 00:07:08,430
Remember the vigilance.

114
00:07:08,430 --> 00:07:12,400
A lonely person is scanning for
social threat, and finding it.

115
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:16,420
Which means a lonely person walks
into a room already braced.

116
00:07:16,420 --> 00:07:18,690
Reads a neutral message as cold.

117
00:07:18,690 --> 00:07:21,630
Notices the pause before somebody answers.

118
00:07:21,630 --> 00:07:24,580
Assumes the invitation was politeness.

119
00:07:24,580 --> 00:07:26,610
And people can feel that.

120
00:07:26,610 --> 00:07:36,150
Somebody who is braced for rejection is harder to be around — not because
they've done anything wrong, but because wariness is contagious and reads as dislike.

121
00:07:36,150 --> 00:07:43,350
So the alarm designed to pull you back towards the group
can, if it runs long enough, quietly push the group away.

122
00:07:43,350 --> 00:07:45,250
Which makes the alarm louder.

123
00:07:45,250 --> 00:07:47,010
Which makes the scanning worse.

124
00:07:47,010 --> 00:07:59,220
Cacioppo's work found exactly this pattern, and it explains something that otherwise makes no sense:
why loneliness so often gets worse in exactly the situations where more people are available.

125
00:07:59,220 --> 00:08:01,220
It isn't a lack of opportunity.

126
00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:07,620
It's a system stuck in emergency mode, doing emergency
things, in a situation that isn't an emergency.

127
00:08:07,620 --> 00:08:13,030
Yes, and this is the part that should make you
take it seriously rather than treat it as a mood.

128
00:08:13,030 --> 00:08:26,170
When researchers pooled dozens of studies covering hundreds of thousands of people, they found that weak social connection predicted
earlier death by a margin big enough that they compared the effect to smoking around fifteen cigarettes a day.

129
00:08:26,170 --> 00:08:34,770
Chronic loneliness shows up in the body as elevated stress
hormones, higher inflammation, worse immune response, higher blood pressure.

130
00:08:34,770 --> 00:08:36,500
Not metaphorically.

131
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:38,240
On the blood tests.

132
00:08:38,240 --> 00:08:45,570
Which is exactly what you'd expect from a system that thinks you
have been separated from the group and are therefore in mortal danger.

133
00:08:45,570 --> 00:08:53,090
It floods you with the chemistry of an emergency, and it does not
stop, because from its point of view the emergency has not ended.

134
00:08:53,090 --> 00:08:57,160
Your body genuinely believes you are in
danger out here on your own.

135
00:08:57,160 --> 00:09:00,530
And in the world it was built
for, it would have been right.

136
00:09:00,530 --> 00:09:04,450
So — did ancient humans get lonely?

137
00:09:04,450 --> 00:09:06,120
They had the equipment.

138
00:09:06,120 --> 00:09:12,100
They must have felt it, in the moments when the
group thinned out, when someone died, when they were separated.

139
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:18,260
But it was rare, it was brief, and it
usually got solved, because everybody was right there.

140
00:09:18,260 --> 00:09:21,990
We kept the alarm and dismantled
the thing it was pointing at.

141
00:09:21,990 --> 00:09:30,830
Which means that when you feel it, you are not malfunctioning, and you are not
weak, and it is not evidence of anything being wrong with you as a person.

142
00:09:30,830 --> 00:09:38,030
It is a two-hundred-thousand-year-old warning light, wired directly into
your body, doing what it has always done.

143
00:09:38,030 --> 00:09:42,220
Telling you that you have drifted from
the group and need to get back.

144
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:45,270
It has never once been
embarrassed to say so.

145
00:09:45,270 --> 00:09:52,520
And if you're now wondering what else your body is shouting about
a world that no longer exists...
...that's a question for another video.
